To find the area of the parallelogram generated by the vectors \(\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\), we need to compute the magnitude of their cross product. The cross product of two vectors \(\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix}\) is given by:

\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
b_1 & b_2 & b_3
\end{vmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
a_2 b_3 - a_3 b_2 \\
a_3 b_1 - a_1 b_3 \\
a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1
\end{pmatrix}
\]

For the vectors \(\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\), the cross product is:

\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
3 & 1 & -2 \\
1 & -3 & 4
\end{vmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
(1)(4) - (-2)(-3) \\
(-2)(1) - (3)(4) \\
(3)(-3) - (1)(1)
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
4 - 6 \\
-2 - 12 \\
-9 - 1
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
-14 \\
-10
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Next, we find the magnitude of this vector:

\[
\| \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} \| = \sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} = \sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} = \sqrt{300} = 10\sqrt{3}
\]

Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is:

\[
\boxed{10\sqrt{3}}
\]

